Oddly enough, I think APL is a pretty decent language, although 
its peculiar character set is, as you say, utterly awful.  It's 
among the languages that have taught me different ways to think 
about problems.  I would not recommend it for a project undertaken 
by more than a half-dozen people, but I would recommend studying 
it to anyone who wants to be able to think more flexibly in code.

Other languages I learned new ways to think from include FORTH, 
Lisp, Scheme (I list Scheme separately from Lisp because a nearly-
pure functional style is practical and succinct in Scheme and I 
never would have learned it from Lisp alone), Smalltalk, Prolog, 
and a few others.  I recommend them.

But for a large project undertaken by a bunch of people who 
don't actually want to learn about programming languages or 
new ways to think in code, stick with something dead standard 
simple like C or C++.  

